-5. Inclusive Practices — Disability, Accessibility, Belonging Without Assumptions
-Disability includes many lived experiences — mobility (cane, wheelchair, chronic pain), sensory (Deaf, hard-of-hearing, low vision, blind), cognitive, mental health, and chronic illness. The ADA requires equitable access for people with disabilities in public programs and accommodations, which often means reasonable modifications, auxiliary aids and services, and accessible facilities [M03-7].
-
-Everyday inclusive practices:
-
-Mobility: Do not move someone’s cane, bag, or wheelchair without permission. Offer extra time and space at kiosks, counters, or waiting areas. If a line is long, offer a seat or a pager/number so they can wait comfortably. Ask: “Would you like extra time or a different way to complete this form?”
-
-Hearing: Do not assume reading ability from Deaf identity, and do not assume everyone signs. Mr. Jackson wrote he can read English and uses ASL — offer options: qualified ASL interpreter (in-person or Video Remote Interpreting), captioned phone, written exchange for simple topics, or real-time captioning for meetings — and ask preference. “We can call Video ASL now, or we can write for scheduling — what works best for you today? Coming back tomorrow is an option, but we have same-day interpreter access if you can wait a few minutes.” [M03-5][M03-7] Many agencies must provide effective communication through appropriate aids and services — “just write back quickly” is often not effective for complex forms [M03-7].
-
-Vision: Offer verbal wayfinding (“The kiosk is 10 steps ahead on your right”), large-print or electronic forms that work with screen readers, and reading assistance if wanted. Do not touch someone or their guide dog without asking.
-
-Belonging: Reflect community in what people see — multilingual signs, diverse images, halal/vegetarian/kosher/vegetarian options noted neutrally, not as “special for her.” When a staff complaint singles out a participant (“change food options for her”), it harms belonging even when the participant overhears it indirectly. Address it privately with a supervisor, and model inclusive language in the same space: “We offer several food options so everyone can eat comfortably — let’s keep the break area respectful.” [M03-1][M03-8]
-
-Ask, don’t assume: “What is most helpful for you today?” is better than guessing needs from one characteristic.
